          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get anxious most days and it interferes with work.",
          "answer": "Feeling anxious most days, and having it get in the way of work, is worth raising with a doctor or other clinician. I can't tell you what is causing it. Only a clinician can assess that.\n\nThe reference material I found describes generalized anxiety disorder as persistent, excessive worry about many everyday matters, occurring more days than not for at least six months, that is hard to control and that affects daily functioning (source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder,\" educational summary). The same source notes that anxiety is normal, and that it becomes a concern when it is out of proportion to the situation, keeps going when circumstances change, and costs a person in their functioning. It also lists common accompanying signs: restlessness, tiring easily, trouble concentrating, irritability, muscle tension and poor sleep. Whether any of this fits your situation is a question for a clinician.\n\nThe results also included a general note on physical activity and anxiety (source: \"Physical activity and anxiety\"). It says that exercise can cause a racing heart and fast breathing, which can feel alarming to an anxious person, and that starting with small, regular activity tends to work better than an ambitious plan that is quickly dropped. The results did not cover treatments or what a clinician would do.\n\nIf your anxiety becomes severe, gets worse, or you have thoughts of harming yourself, seek medical help promptly. If you feel you might act on such thoughts, contact emergency services right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
