          "session_id": "full-mt-m-013",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there a questionnaire they use for it?",
          "answer": "Yes. The reference material describes a seven-item questionnaire for generalized anxiety disorder, often called the GAD-7. It is the instrument most often used both to screen for anxiety and to track how severe it is (source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care,\" preventive services and psychiatric association guidance).\n\nThe same source gives rough severity bands: 5 for mild, 10 for moderate and 15 for severe. A score of 10 is the point at which further assessment usually follows. These numbers are for reference only. A questionnaire result is not a diagnosis, and a clinician is the one who interprets it. The material does not say what the questions are, so I can't tell you what the questionnaire asks.\n\nThe same source also covers screening guidance. Preventive-services guidance describes screening adults for anxiety disorders where systems are in place to ensure accurate diagnosis and follow-up, with the strongest statement for adults under 65. For older adults, the evidence was judged insufficient to make a recommendation either way. That is a statement about the evidence, not a recommendation against screening.\n\nThe results did not cover where to get the questionnaire, how it is scored in practice, or what treatments follow. A doctor or other clinician can talk you through those.\n\nIf your anxiety becomes severe, gets worse, or you have thoughts of harming yourself, seek medical help promptly. If you feel you might act on such thoughts, contact emergency services right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
